{"name":"Spirit Dance","hex":"#514b80","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"spirit","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The soul of a person or other creature.","A supernatural being, often but not exclusively without physical form; ghost, fairy, angel."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To carry off, especially in haste, secrecy, or mystery.","Sometimes followed by up: to animate with vigour; to excite; to encourage; to inspirit."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spirit"},{"term":"dance","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sequence of rhythmic steps or movements usually performed to music, for pleasure or as a form of social interaction.","A social gathering where dancing is the main activity."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move with rhythmic steps or movements, especially in time to music.","To leap or move lightly and rapidly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dance"}],"entries":[{"title":"Spirit Dance (Michael White album)","blocks":[{"text":"Spirit Dance is the debut album by American violinist and composer Michael White featuring performances recorded in 1971 and released on the Impulse! label."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/8f/Spirit_Dance.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spirit_Dance_(Michael_White_album)","wikidata":"Q7577870"},{"title":"Native American religions","blocks":[{"text":"Native American religions, Native American faith, or American Indian religions are the Indigenous spiritual practices of the Indigenous peoples of the Americas. Ceremonial practices involve sacred, spiritual, cultural traditions that can differ slightly among the indigenous groups of the continent. Early European explorers describe some communities, now labelled Native American tribes in the United States and small First Nations or bands in Canada, as each having unique religious practices. The religions' theologies may be monotheistic, polytheistic, henotheistic, animistic, shamanistic, pantheistic, or any combination thereof, among others. Traditional beliefs are usually passed down in the oral tradition forms of myths, oral histories, stories, allegories, and principles. Nowadays, as scholars note, many American Natives are having a renewed interest in their own traditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Three_Navajo_men_proceeding_as_war_gods._Wellcome_V0038486.jpg/330px-Three_Navajo_men_proceeding_as_war_gods._Wellcome_V0038486.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Native_American_religions","wikidata":"Q5162345"},{"title":"Dance","blocks":[{"text":"Dance is an art form, consisting of sequences of body movements with aesthetic and often symbolic value, either improvised or purposefully selected. Dance can be categorized and described by its choreography, by its repertoire of movements or by its historical period or place of origin. Dance is typically performed with musical accompaniment, and sometimes with the dancer simultaneously using a musical instrument themselves."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Two_dancers.jpg/330px-Two_dancers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dance","wikidata":"Q11639"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Big_Horn_Medicine_Wheel_Sunrise_2011.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Big_Horn_Medicine_Wheel_Sunrise_2011.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Big_Horn_Medicine_Wheel_Sunrise_2011.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Big_Horn_Medicine_Wheel_Sunrise_2011.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/comm